# Mutation cases for "how much of Kotlin's divergence list the build-time seam
# buys back".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/the_lowering_dividend_is_derived_not_declared.cases`.
#
# `tests/ecmascript/kotlin_lua_divergences.json` lists the cases this backend's
# Lua engine answers differently from ECMA-262, all of them on one path because
# `runtime-rewriter` is the only route the generated Kotlin has. What the list
# could not say is what the OTHER route already answers, which is the question
# anybody deciding whether to fund the lowered seam asks first: how many of
# these would stop existing if translation moved to build time?
#
# ⚠ **The answer is not written here.** It lives in that file's
# `build_time_frontend.measured`, `kotlin_lowering_dividend` re-derives it on
# every push, and it moved twice on 2026-08-29 alone. The reason it is worth a
# casefile is that it is
# the exact shape of number this repository has been wrong about four times —
# "27 of its 58" over a table of 98, a `58/58` scoreboard, a gate's own comment
# saying "58-case", a divergence list's prose naming an entry its array did not
# hold. Every one of them was a count that lived somewhere nothing re-derived.
#
# So the verdicts are not judged here, they are DERIVED — each from
# `kotlin_lowered_ecma262.json`, which `LoweredEcma262Test` holds against the
# running Lua engine in both directions. These cases are the control on that
# derivation: they model the four ways the number could go back to being typed,
# plus the day the field should stop existing.
#
# ⚠ The sixth round could not buy this control: its suite is Gradle's and
# `scripts/mutate` has no Gradle runner, so its two reds were applied and
# reverted by hand. This lane is `cargo`, which is why the same reasoning gets
# a durable casefile here.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --test kotlin_lowering_dividend
mutation_targets sce-build/src/generator.rs
mutation_runtime_targets tests/ecmascript/kotlin_lua_divergences.json docs/SCE_LUA_TRANSLATION_SEAM.md

# ── A verdict is talked into the exempting value ───────────────────
#
# `unmeasured` is the one verdict that takes an entry OUT of the measurement:
# it says the lowered route cannot ask this case at all. So it is the value a
# label drifts toward when somebody wants a disagreement to go away, and the
# derivation is what makes that impossible — the case is either declared
# `unreachable` in the lowered file, where `LoweredEcma262Test` defends it in
# both directions against the engine, or it is not.

mutation_case "a verdict is talked into the exempting value" <<'PY'
edit("tests/ecmascript/kotlin_lua_divergences.json",
     '{ "source": "!a", "clause": "12.5.9 logical NOT", "diverges_on": ["runtime-rewriter"], "build_time_frontend": "answers" }',
     '{ "source": "!a", "clause": "12.5.9 logical NOT", "diverges_on": ["runtime-rewriter"], "build_time_frontend": "unmeasured" }')
PY

# ── The field becomes quietly optional ─────────────────────────────
#
# An entry with no verdict is not an entry with no answer: the value a missing
# field silently means is `unmeasured`, because that is what "this lane did not
# measure it" reads as. An escape hatch reached by OMISSION is one nobody has
# to justify, which is the failure `diverges_on` already refuses one key above
# this one. Unclassified is red, not a default.

mutation_case "the verdict field is quietly optional" <<'PY'
edit("tests/ecmascript/kotlin_lua_divergences.json",
     '"clause": "12.5.9 logical NOT", "diverges_on": ["runtime-rewriter"], "build_time_frontend": "answers" }',
     '"clause": "12.5.9 logical NOT", "diverges_on": ["runtime-rewriter"] }')
PY

# ── The tally drifts from the list it summarises ───────────────────
#
# The count is what a reader takes away, and it is stored beside the list
# rather than computed by whoever reads it — which is exactly the arrangement
# that was wrong every previous time. It survives only while something
# re-counts the entries under it.
#
# ⚠ **The anchor is DERIVED, and that is a repair.** Until 2026-08-30 this case
# spelled `"answers": 39` literally, and on 2026-08-29 the tally moved 39 → 44
# in three commits. The anchor then matched nothing, the harness reported
# INCONCLUSIVE, and `Mutation Rounds` was red on `ef6513194b` — a casefile whose
# whole subject is "a count that lived somewhere nothing re-derived" had itself
# become one. The body reads the number out of the file and mutates THAT, so the
# case follows the tally wherever it goes and stays about the clause instead of
# about a particular value.

mutation_case "the stated tally drifts from the entries under it" <<'PY'
import re

PATH = "tests/ecmascript/kotlin_lua_divergences.json"
found = re.search(r'"measured": \{ "answers": (\d+),', open(PATH).read())
if not found:
    raise SystemExit(
        "%s no longer states a `measured.answers` tally in the shape this case "
        "mutates. That field IS the clause under test, so its disappearance is a "
        "finding, not something to work around." % PATH
    )
edit(PATH, found.group(0),
     '"measured": { "answers": %d,' % (int(found.group(1)) + 1))
PY

# ── The document keeps a number the tree has moved past ────────────
#
# The lists are read by suites; the seam document is read by whoever is
# deciding what to build next, so it is the copy most worth holding to the tree
# and the least likely to be re-derived by hand. This is the failure that
# actually happened to the sentence this table replaces — "the frontend answers
# all 98 cases" outlived the measurement it was made from.

#
# ⚠ Derived for the same reason the case above it is, and it went stale on the
# same day: the document's row said 39 while the list said 44.

mutation_case "the seam document keeps a number the list has moved past" <<'PY'
import re

PATH = "docs/SCE_LUA_TRANSLATION_SEAM.md"
found = re.search(r"\| `answers` \| (\d+) \|", open(PATH).read())
if not found:
    raise SystemExit(
        "%s no longer carries the `answers` row of the dividend table. That row "
        "IS the clause under test — the document's copy of a number the list "
        "owns — so its disappearance is a finding." % PATH
    )
edit(PATH, found.group(0), "| `answers` | %d |" % (int(found.group(1)) + 1))
PY

# ── A verdict this lane derives is not a spelling the file admits ──
#
# The vocabulary is declared in the file rather than only in the lane, the same
# way `paths` is, so that neither side can hold a value the other cannot. A
# renamed key leaves the lane deriving `unmeasured` for five entries the file
# would then reject as misspellings — and, worse in the other direction, leaves
# a declared value nothing can ever assign.

mutation_case "the declared vocabulary drops a value the lane derives" <<'PY'
edit("tests/ecmascript/kotlin_lua_divergences.json",
     '"unmeasured": "The case cannot be put',
     '"unmeasurable": "The case cannot be put')
PY

# ── The seam opens and the field survives it ───────────────────────
#
# This is the case for the assertion that has nothing to measure today, and it
# is the one that keeps `build_time_frontend` from becoming permanent. The
# field exists ONLY because `build-time-lowering` is not yet a path this
# backend has, so the answer cannot be written where it belongs — in
# `diverges_on`, beside the path it is about. Opening the Lua target makes the
# field a second vocabulary for a fact `diverges_on` can now carry, and two
# spellings of one fact drift.
#
# Mutating the generator rather than the lane's own helper on purpose: the
# condition being modelled is the real one, and `mutation_tests` names this
# binary alone, so the red that comes back is this gate's and not another
# suite's opinion about the same edit.

mutation_case "the seam opens and the field survives as a second vocabulary" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/generator.rs",
     """            ScriptEngineTarget::Lua => {
                self.unmigrated_expression_sites().is_empty()
                    && self.unclassified_expression_sites().is_empty()
            }""",
     """            ScriptEngineTarget::Lua => true,""")
PY
